{"name":"Vintage Porcelain","hex":"#f2edec","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"vintage","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The yield of grapes or wine from a vineyard or district during one season.","Wine, especially high-quality, identified as to year and vineyard or district of origin."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or relating to a vintage, or to wine identified by a specific vintage.","Having an enduring appeal; high-quality."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/vintage"},{"term":"porcelain","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard white translucent ceramic, originally made by firing kaolin, quartz, and feldspar at high temperatures but now also inclusive of similar artificial materials; also often  such a material as a symbol of the fragility, elegance, etc. traditionally associated with porcelain goods.","Synonym of china: porcelain tableware."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To coat with a porcelain enamel."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/porcelain"}],"entries":[{"title":"Vintage","blocks":[{"text":"In winemaking, vintage is the process of picking grapes to create wine. A vintage wine is one made from grapes that were all, or primarily, grown and harvested in a single specified year. In certain wines, it can denote quality, as in Port wine, where Port houses make and declare vintage Port in their best years. From this tradition, a common, though not strictly correct, usage applies the term to any wine that is perceived to be particularly old or of a particularly high quality."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Cup_vintage_Cdm_Paris_320_n1.jpg/330px-Cup_vintage_Cdm_Paris_320_n1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vintage"},{"title":"Porcelain","blocks":[{"text":"Porcelain, also called china, is a ceramic material made by heating raw materials, generally including kaolinite, in a kiln to temperatures between 1,200 and 1,400 °C. The greater strength and translucence of porcelain, relative to other types of pottery, arise mainly from vitrification and the formation of the mineral mullite within the body at these high temperatures. End applications include tableware, decorative ware such as figurines, and products in technology and industry such as electrical insulators and laboratory ware."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/%E6%B1%9D%E7%AA%AF_%E9%9D%92%E7%93%B7%E8%86%BD%E7%93%B6.png/330px-%E6%B1%9D%E7%AA%AF_%E9%9D%92%E7%93%B7%E8%86%BD%E7%93%B6.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Porcelain"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/The_Vintagers_after_a_Miniature_of_the_Dialogues_de_Saint_Gregoire_Thirteenth_Century_Manuscript_of_the_Royal_Library_of_Brussels.png/500px-The_Vintagers_after_a_Miniature_of_the_Dialogues_de_Saint_Gregoire_Thirteenth_Century_Manuscript_of_the_Royal_Library_of_Brussels.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/The_Vintagers_after_a_Miniature_of_the_Dialogues_de_Saint_Gregoire_Thirteenth_Century_Manuscript_of_the_Royal_Library_of_Brussels.png/1280px-The_Vintagers_after_a_Miniature_of_the_Dialogues_de_Saint_Gregoire_Thirteenth_Century_Manuscript_of_the_Royal_Library_of_Brussels.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/The_Vintagers_after_a_Miniature_of_the_Dialogues_de_Saint_Gregoire_Thirteenth_Century_Manuscript_of_the_Royal_Library_of_Brussels.png/500px-The_Vintagers_after_a_Miniature_of_the_Dialogues_de_Saint_Gregoire_Thirteenth_Century_Manuscript_of_the_Royal_Library_of_Brussels.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Vintagers, after a miniature of the \"Dialogues de Saint Gregoire\" (13th century)—manuscript of the Royal Library of Belgium","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Vintagers_after_a_Miniature_of_the_Dialogues_de_Saint_Gregoire_Thirteenth_Century_Manuscript_of_the_Royal_Library_of_Brussels.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Jacob_Philipp_Hackert_003.jpg/500px-Jacob_Philipp_Hackert_003.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Jacob_Philipp_Hackert_003.jpg/1280px-Jacob_Philipp_Hackert_003.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Jacob_Philipp_Hackert_003.jpg/500px-Jacob_Philipp_Hackert_003.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Vintage near Sorrento, Italy, Jacob Philipp Hackert, c. 1784","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJacob_Philipp_Hackert_003.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Champagne_mill%C3%A9sim%C3%A9_1995.JPG/500px-Champagne_mill%C3%A9sim%C3%A9_1995.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Champagne_mill%C3%A9sim%C3%A9_1995.JPG/1280px-Champagne_mill%C3%A9sim%C3%A9_1995.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Champagne_mill%C3%A9sim%C3%A9_1995.JPG/500px-Champagne_mill%C3%A9sim%C3%A9_1995.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Vintage champagne","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChampagne_mill%C3%A9sim%C3%A9_1995.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Vendanges_-_c%C3%B4tes_de_Beaune.JPG/500px-Vendanges_-_c%C3%B4tes_de_Beaune.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedi